from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Who is the main audience for a startup pitch?","options":["only paying customers","investors, partners, potential hires, and sometimes customers","only newspaper reporters","only tax authorities"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You pitch to anyone whose support you need: investors, future teammates, partners, judges, and often early customers."},{"prompt":"TAM, SAM, and SOM on a market-size slide describe...","options":["three kinds of revenue","three stages of hiring","layers of market size, from the total market down to the share you can realistically win","three legal filings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TAM is the total market, SAM is the part you can serve, and SOM is the slice you can obtain in the near term."},{"prompt":"About how long should an elevator pitch last?","options":["30 to 60 seconds","about 15 minutes","roughly one hour","a full afternoon"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An elevator pitch is a 30 to 60 second summary you can deliver in the time of a short lift ride."},{"prompt":"The slide that states how much money you want to raise and what you will spend it on is called...","options":["the team slide","the ask","the traction slide","the appendix"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ask names the amount you are raising and the milestones that money will reach."},{"prompt":"A strong pitch narrative usually opens with...","options":["the detailed financial model","a relatable problem","the list of shareholders","a legal disclaimer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Starting with a clear, human problem pulls the listener in before you reveal your solution."},{"prompt":"Traction on a pitch deck means...","options":["your choice of logo colors","evidence that people are already using, paying for, or signing up for your product","the number of slides you have","the city your office is in"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Traction is proof of real demand: revenue, active users, growth rate, waitlists, or signed deals."},{"prompt":"When an investor asks a hard question you cannot fully answer, the best move is to...","options":["pretend you know the answer","answer honestly, share what you do know, and promise a precise follow-up","quickly change the subject","end the meeting early"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Honesty plus a concrete follow-up builds trust; bluffing almost always backfires."},{"prompt":"Demo day is best described as...","options":["the day you fire your first employee","an event where several startups pitch on stage to a room of investors","a public product return window","an internal code review"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Demo day is a showcase, often at the end of an accelerator, where founders pitch to many investors at once."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Why pitching matters and who you pitch to', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why pitching is a core founder skill rather than an occasional chore, name the main audiences a startup pitches to, and match each audience to the single thing it most wants to hear. By the end of this lesson you will be able to size up a listener in one sentence and open with the hook that fits them. Warm-up, using the diagnostic you just finished: besides investors, write down three groups who might sit across the table when you pitch, and for each one guess the first question running through their mind. Keep that short list beside you while you read. The rest of the lesson gives you a fast way to read any room and lead with the point that room cares about, so you stop giving the same speech to everyone and start adjusting on the spot. That single habit separates founders who get a second meeting from founders who do not."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A pitch is a short, structured case for why your company deserves someone''s time, money, or trust. Founders pitch far more often than they expect to. You pitch an investor for funding, but you also pitch a talented engineer to leave a safe job, a landlord to rent you space, a journalist to write a story, and a first customer to take a chance on an unknown brand. Each of these people listens through a different filter. An investor weighs return against risk and wants to believe the market is large and growing. A customer only cares whether you solve a real pain at a fair price. A candidate wants to know the mission matters and that joining early will pay off. A partner looks for mutual benefit and reach. The story can stay the same, but the emphasis must move. Pitching well is therefore less about memorizing a speech and more about translation: taking one truth about your company and saying it in the language of the person in front of you."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Picture one product, a simple scheduling tool for small clinics, pitched to three audiences. To an investor you say: clinics lose money on missed appointments, there are hundreds of thousands of them, and our reminders already cut no-shows by a fifth, so this is a large market with proven results. To a clinic owner you drop the market talk entirely and say: your front desk spends two hours a day on phone reminders, and we do it automatically for less than one no-show costs you per week. To a future lead engineer you say: healthcare software is slow and clunky, we are a small team rebuilding it the right way, and you would own the whole reminders system from day one. Same company, three openings. Notice what changed: the investor heard market and traction, the customer heard time and money saved, and the engineer heard problem and ownership. The core facts never changed; only the lead did. A good exercise is to write your own three openers before any important meeting."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer each, then check yourself. 1) Name two audiences a founder might pitch besides investors. 2) An investor and a customer hear the same pitch differently - what does each one most want to know? 3) True or false: you should give every audience the identical speech. 4) A candidate is deciding whether to join your five-person startup. What two things should your pitch stress? 5) Rewrite this investor line for a customer: ''We address a two billion dollar market with strong early traction.'' Answers: 1) Any two of customers, potential hires, partners, journalists, accelerator judges, landlords, or suppliers. 2) The investor wants return and market size; the customer wants the specific pain you solve and the price. 3) False - keep the facts but change the emphasis for each listener. 4) That the mission matters and that joining early carries real ownership and upside. 5) Something like: ''You are losing customers every week to this problem, and we fix it today for a price that pays for itself.''"},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the pitch as a performance about yourself instead of a case about the listener''s gain, so every sentence starts with ''we'' and ''our'' rather than ''you'' and ''your.'' Mistake: using one frozen script for every audience, which means the investor hears customer detail and the customer hears market jargon, and both tune out. Mistake: forgetting that recruiting and selling are pitches too, so founders practice only the funding version and freeze when a candidate asks why they should join. Fix all three by writing one short opener per audience before the meeting. Exit ticket: (a) In one sentence, why is pitching a core founder skill and not just something you do to raise money? (b) Name the one thing a customer most wants to hear in a pitch. Answers: (a) Because founders constantly persuade investors, hires, partners, and customers, and every one of those conversations is a pitch. (b) The specific problem you solve for them, and what it costs."}]}'::jsonb, 'A pitch is a case, not a brag. Lead with what the listener gains, not with how clever you are.', 11),
  (3, 'Storytelling and narrative structure for startups', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Shape the facts about your startup into a story that a listener remembers and repeats, using a simple narrative arc you can reuse for any audience. By the end you will be able to take a dry feature list and turn it into a problem, a stakes line, a turning point, and a resolution. Warm-up: think of the last product you recommended to a friend. You almost certainly did not list its features - you told a tiny story about a problem it fixed for you. Jot down that story in two sentences. People forget numbers within minutes but remember stories for years, and investors hear dozens of pitches a week, so the one that sticks in memory is the one that gets funded. This lesson gives you the skeleton that makes your pitch stick, and shows where your hard data belongs inside it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A pitch is still an argument, but the most persuasive arguments are carried by story. A reliable startup arc has four beats. First, the problem: a specific, relatable pain that a real person feels. Second, the stakes: why that pain matters and what it costs if nothing changes, which creates tension. Third, the turning point: your solution arrives and changes what is possible. Fourth, the resolution: the better world your customer now lives in, backed by proof that it is already happening. A crucial rule runs underneath all four beats: the customer is the hero, and your company is the helpful guide, not the star. Founders instinctively cast themselves and their clever technology as the hero, but listeners do not fall in love with your product. They fall in love with a version of themselves or their business that your product makes possible. Keep the customer center stage, and your data, your demo, and your team become supporting evidence for a change the listener can already picture."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Take the clinic scheduling tool again and run it through the arc. Problem: Maria runs a small dental clinic, and every week a handful of patients forget their appointments. Stakes: each empty chair costs her roughly two hundred dollars, her front desk burns hours on reminder calls, and she cannot grow because the schedule is never full. That is the tension - we feel Maria''s frustration. Turning point: she installs a tool that sends patients a friendly reminder and a one-tap confirmation, so the manual calling stops. Resolution: no-shows drop by a fifth, the front desk gets two hours back a day, and Maria books the newly free slots - and, we add, three hundred other clinics have seen the same result. Notice how the proof lands at the end, as the payoff to a story, rather than as a cold statistic at the start. The numbers now mean something because we met Maria first. Build your own arc by naming a real customer, their pain, the cost of that pain, your turn, and the proof."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) Name the four beats of the startup narrative arc in order. 2) In a good pitch story, who is the hero? 3) Why place your strongest data at the resolution rather than the opening? 4) What is the purpose of the stakes beat? 5) Rewrite this feature-first line as a problem-first opener: ''Our tool uses automated messaging to reduce missed appointments.'' Answers: 1) Problem, stakes, turning point, resolution. 2) The customer, not you or your product. 3) Because a number means little until the listener cares about the person it describes; the story earns the data, so proof lands hardest at the end. 4) To create tension by showing what the problem costs if nothing changes, so the listener actively wants the solution rather than merely nodding along. 5) Something like: ''Every week, clinics lose money to patients who simply forget they had an appointment.'' Notice that the rewrite names a real cost and a human slip, while the original only described a feature in search of a reason to exist."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: opening with your company and its features, so the listener meets your product before they feel any problem and has no reason to care. Mistake: making your technology the hero, which flatters you but leaves the customer with no one to root for. Mistake: skipping the stakes, so there is no tension and the solution feels like a nice-to-have rather than a rescue. Mistake: burying a great result in the middle of a slide instead of landing it as the payoff. Fix these by writing the problem and stakes first, casting the customer as hero, and saving your best proof for last. Exit ticket: (a) What single change turns a feature list into a story? (b) Where in the arc does your proof belong, and why? Answers: (a) Lead with a real person''s problem and its cost before you mention your product. (b) At the resolution, because by then the listener cares about the person, so the proof carries weight."}]}'::jsonb, 'The customer is the hero of your story; your product is the tool that helps them win, not the star.', 12),
  (4, 'The pitch deck and its standard slides', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"List the standard slides of a startup pitch deck in a sensible order and say in one line what each slide must prove. By the end you will be able to sketch a ten to twelve slide outline for any company and know exactly what belongs on each page. Warm-up: from memory, name every slide you think a deck should contain, then compare your list to the one in this lesson and mark what you missed. Most first decks are missing the same three slides, and you will likely find yours among them. A pitch deck is the visual backbone of a longer pitch - a short, ordered set of slides, usually ten to fifteen, that walks an investor from the problem all the way to your request for money. Knowing the standard order means you never leave an investor wondering about something you simply forgot to cover."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The slide order is not a rigid law, but a widely shared convention exists because it answers an investor''s questions in the order they naturally arise. A common sequence runs: problem, solution, product, market size, business model, go-to-market, traction, competition, team, financials, and the ask. First you establish a real problem, then show your solution and the product that delivers it. Next you prove the opportunity is big with market size, usually framed as TAM, SAM, and SOM. Then you explain how you make money with the business model, and how you reach customers with go-to-market. Traction shows the plan is already working, competition shows you understand the field and why you win, and team shows you are the right people to pull it off. Financials project where the numbers go, and the ask closes by naming how much you are raising and what it buys. Each slide answers one question, and together they form a complete argument."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Look closely at three slides founders most often get wrong. Market size uses three layers: TAM is the Total Addressable Market, the full demand if everyone who could buy did; SAM is the Serviceable Available Market, the slice your product and region can actually serve; and SOM is the Serviceable Obtainable Market, the realistic share you can win in a few years. For the clinic tool, TAM might be all clinics worldwide, SAM might be dental clinics in your country, and SOM might be the few thousand you can reach through your sales plan. The competition slide should never say ''we have no competitors'' - that signals either no market or no homework. Instead show a simple grid of rivals and the one dimension where you clearly win. The ask should be concrete: not ''we are raising money,'' but ''we are raising one million dollars to reach fifty thousand users and twelve months of runway.'' Specific numbers signal that you have thought it through."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) Put these in the usual order: team, problem, traction, solution. 2) What do TAM, SAM, and SOM stand for? 3) Which slide names the amount you are raising and its use? 4) Why is ''we have no competitors'' a weak thing to put on the competition slide? 5) Name the slide that shows your plan is already working. Answers: 1) Problem, solution, traction, team. 2) Total Addressable Market, Serviceable Available Market, and Serviceable Obtainable Market. 3) The ask. 4) It suggests either that no real market exists or that you have not researched the field; every product competes with something, even the status quo, including a spreadsheet or a pen and paper. 5) The traction slide, which proves people already use, pay for, or sign up for your product. As a bonus check, name the slide that comes directly after market size in the usual order - it is the business model, where you explain who pays you and how, and it should always follow proof that the market is large enough to be worth selling into."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: cramming several ideas onto one slide, which forces the investor to read instead of listen to you. Mistake: inflating market size by presenting only the huge TAM and skipping SAM and SOM, so the number feels unreal. Mistake: hiding or omitting the competition slide, which reads as naive rather than confident. Mistake: ending with a vague ask, leaving the investor unsure what you actually want. Fix these with one idea per slide, an honest three-layer market, a candid competition grid, and a specific ask with a number and a purpose. Exit ticket: (a) Name the standard slide that comes right before the ask. (b) In one sentence, what must the market-size slide prove? Answers: (a) Financials. (b) That the opportunity is large enough to matter, shown realistically through TAM, SAM, and SOM rather than one inflated figure."}]}'::jsonb, 'One idea per slide. If a slide needs two headlines, it is really two slides.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Besides investors, a founder commonly pitches to...","options":["no one else","potential hires, partners, and customers","only competitors","only accountants"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recruiting, selling, and forming partnerships are all pitches aimed at different audiences."},{"prompt":"In a well-built startup story, the hero should be...","options":["the founder","the product","the customer","the investor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The customer is the hero; your company is the guide that helps them win."},{"prompt":"The four beats of the startup narrative arc are...","options":["price, features, logo, ask","problem, stakes, turning point, resolution","intro, body, conclusion, appendix","hello, demo, thanks, goodbye"],"answer":1,"explanation":"You set up a problem, raise the stakes, introduce the turning point, and show the resolution with proof."},{"prompt":"SOM stands for...","options":["Standard Operating Model","Serviceable Obtainable Market","Scale Of Marketing","Sum Of Margins"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SOM is the Serviceable Obtainable Market, the realistic share you can win in the near term."},{"prompt":"Which ordering follows the common deck convention?","options":["ask, team, problem, solution","problem, solution, market size, traction","financials, logo, problem, thanks","traction, ask, problem, team"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A typical deck moves from problem and solution to market size and then traction before closing."},{"prompt":"Putting ''we have no competitors'' on your competition slide usually signals...","options":["a strong moat","that there is no market or no research","a patent","high revenue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every product competes with something, even the status quo, so claiming none reads as naive."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'The elevator pitch', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Write and deliver a thirty to sixty second elevator pitch that makes a stranger understand what you do and want to hear more. By the end you will have a repeatable formula and your own first draft. Warm-up: imagine you step into a lift and a well-known investor asks what you are building. You have the length of one short ride before the doors open. What would you say right now, with no slides and no notes? Say it out loud and time yourself. Most founders either freeze or ramble past a full minute, and both lose the listener. The elevator pitch is the smallest, most portable version of your whole story, and because chance meetings cannot be scheduled, you need it memorized and ready. Mastering this short form also sharpens your longer pitch, because it forces you to find the single clearest sentence about what you do."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An elevator pitch is a thirty to sixty second spoken summary, named for the idea that you should be able to deliver it in the time of a lift ride. Its only job is to earn the next conversation, not to close a deal. A clear, reliable formula has four parts you can fill in for any company. One: name the customer and their problem in plain words. Two: state what you do, in one sentence, with no jargon. Three: give one line of proof or difference, such as a result, a number, or what makes you unlike the obvious alternative. Four: end with a simple hook or request, such as an offer to show a demo or a question that invites a reply. The whole thing should sound like something you would actually say to a friend, not like text read from a slide. Short, concrete, and human beats polished and vague every time."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Here is the formula filled in for the clinic tool, spoken in about thirty seconds: ''You know how small clinics lose money every week because patients forget their appointments? We send patients an automatic reminder and a one-tap confirmation, so the front desk stops making calls and the schedule stays full. Three hundred clinics use us already, and they have cut no-shows by about a fifth. I would love to show you how it works in two minutes.'' Break it down: the first line is the customer and problem, the second is what we do in plain speech, the third is proof, and the last is the hook. Notice there is no jargon, no mention of our funding round, and no list of features - just one clear thread a listener can repeat to someone else an hour later. That repeatability is the real test. If the person you pitched can describe your company accurately to a third party, your elevator pitch works."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) About how long should an elevator pitch be? 2) Name the four parts of the formula in order. 3) What is the one goal of an elevator pitch? 4) True or false: you should pack in as many features as you can. 5) Spot the problem in this pitch: ''We are a next-generation cloud-native platform leveraging advanced optimization to synergize workflows.'' Answers: 1) Thirty to sixty seconds. 2) Customer and problem, what you do, proof or difference, and a hook or request. 3) To earn the next conversation, not to close a deal on the spot. 4) False - one clear sentence beats a feature list, because the listener must be able to repeat it. 5) It is all jargon and names no customer, no problem, and no concrete result, so a listener learns nothing and cannot repeat it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: rambling well past a minute, so the listener loses the thread before you reach the point. Mistake: opening with your company name and category instead of the customer''s problem, which gives the listener nothing to care about. Mistake: hiding behind buzzwords, which sound impressive and mean nothing. Mistake: trying to close the deal in the lift rather than simply earning a follow-up. Fix these by timing yourself, leading with the problem, cutting every word of jargon, and ending with a light request. Exit ticket: (a) What is the single job of an elevator pitch? (b) Which part of the formula should come first, and why? Answers: (a) To earn the next conversation. (b) The customer and their problem, because it gives the listener an immediate reason to care before you say what you do."}]}'::jsonb, 'If you cannot say it in one breath without a slide, it is not an elevator pitch yet.', 10),
  (7, 'Designing clear, readable slides', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Design pitch slides that an investor can grasp in a few seconds, by controlling how much goes on each slide, how you use words versus visuals, and how you guide the eye. By the end you will be able to take a crowded slide and cut it down to one clear point. Warm-up: picture the worst presentation slide you have ever seen - probably a wall of tiny text that the speaker then read aloud to you. Note two things that made it painful. You are about to learn the rules that prevent exactly that. Slide design is not decoration; a cluttered slide forces the audience to choose between reading and listening, and they cannot do both, so your words get lost. Clear slides keep attention on you, where it belongs, and make your argument easy to follow and hard to misread."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The governing rule of slide design is one idea per slide. Each slide should make a single point, stated in a short headline the audience can read in a glance, with the rest of the slide supporting that one point. Favor visuals over paragraphs: a simple chart, a product screenshot, or one large number lands faster than a block of text. Keep the word count low, use a type size large enough to read from the back of a room, and leave generous empty space so the eye knows where to rest. Be consistent, so every slide shares the same fonts, colors, and layout and the deck feels like one object rather than a pile of pages. Remember that the slide is a backdrop for you, the speaker. If a slide can be read and fully understood without you, you are reading a document aloud, not giving a pitch. The audience should be listening to your voice while the slide quietly reinforces the point."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Compare two versions of a traction slide. The weak version is a paragraph: ''Since launching in January we have grown steadily and now serve many clinics across the country with strong monthly growth and good retention and positive word of mouth.'' The audience has to read it, and nothing stands out. The strong version shows one line chart of users climbing month over month, a short headline that says ''Up and to the right: 300 clinics in 9 months,'' and nothing else. The eye lands on the rising line, the headline confirms it in four words, and you fill in the detail out loud. That is one idea, one visual, one headline. Apply the same cut everywhere: the market slide becomes three nested circles for TAM, SAM, and SOM instead of three paragraphs; the problem slide becomes one striking statistic or a single customer quote. Every time you are tempted to add a second idea to a slide, split it into two slides instead."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) State the single most important rule of slide design. 2) Should you generally prefer a paragraph or a visual to make a point? 3) If a slide is readable and fully clear with no speaker present, what has gone wrong? 4) Name two things that should stay consistent across every slide. 5) You have a slide with two separate headlines. What should you do? Answers: 1) One idea per slide. 2) A visual, such as a chart, screenshot, or single large number, because it lands faster than a block of text. 3) You have written a document to read aloud rather than a backdrop that supports a spoken pitch, so the audience reads ahead and stops listening to you. 4) Any two of fonts, colors, and layout. 5) Split it into two slides, since two headlines mean two ideas, and each idea deserves its own moment. One more quick check: if someone past the back row cannot read your type, the slide is too dense or the font is too small, and the fix is fewer words at a larger size, not a cleverer arrangement of the same clutter."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: packing a slide with text and then reading it aloud, which bores the room and makes you redundant. Mistake: tiny fonts and dense charts that no one past the second row can read. Mistake: a different look on every slide, so the deck feels thrown together. Mistake: decoration for its own sake - stock images and busy backgrounds that fight the message. Fix these with one idea per slide, large type, visuals over paragraphs, and a single consistent template. Exit ticket: (a) Who should the audience be paying attention to during your pitch, the slide or you? (b) What is the fastest way to fix a slide that has two ideas on it? Answers: (a) You, the speaker; the slide only supports you. (b) Split it into two slides so each carries a single idea."}]}'::jsonb, 'Slides support the speaker; they do not replace the speaker. The audience should listen to you, not read ahead of you.', 11),
  (8, 'Delivering with confidence and handling the Q&A', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Deliver your pitch with steady confidence and handle the question-and-answer session that follows without being thrown off. By the end you will have practical techniques for your voice, your body, and the moment an investor pushes back. Warm-up: recall a time you spoke in front of a group and felt nervous. What did your body do - did you rush, fidget, or lose your place? Name one habit you would like to control. Delivery matters because investors are not only judging your idea; they are judging whether they trust you to lead through hard times, and a shaky, defensive delivery undercuts even a great deck. The good news is that confidence on stage is mostly a set of learnable habits - preparation, pacing, and a calm way of fielding questions - rather than a personality you are born with or without."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Confident delivery rests on a few repeatable habits. Prepare until the structure is second nature, so you can speak to each slide without reading it; rehearsal is what frees you to sound natural. Slow down - nervous founders rush, and a deliberate pace signals calm and command. Make eye contact, stand with an open posture, and let your hands rest unless they are making a point. Then comes the part founders dread most, the Q&A. Reframe it: questions are a sign of interest, and the discussion is often where deals are actually won or lost. When a question comes, listen to the whole thing before answering, pause for a moment to think, and answer directly rather than dodging. If you do not know, say so plainly, share what you do know, and offer to follow up with a precise answer - this builds far more trust than a confident bluff. Stay calm even when a question feels like a challenge, because how you handle pressure is itself part of what is being judged."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Imagine an investor cuts in: ''Your churn looks high. Why would I believe this retains?'' A weak founder gets defensive, talks faster, and insists the number is fine. A strong founder pauses, then says: ''Fair question. Our early churn was high because we onboarded anyone who signed up, including clinics that were not a fit. Over the last three months, since we tightened onboarding, churn has dropped by half, and I can send you the cohort chart after this.'' Notice the pattern: acknowledge the question, answer honestly, show the trend, and offer proof. Another example - you are asked something you simply do not know, like a precise figure for a segment you have not measured. Rather than guessing, say: ''I do not have that number in front of me, but here is how we would find it, and I will send you the exact figure tomorrow.'' In both cases you stayed calm, stayed honest, and turned a hard moment into a display of how you think under pressure."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) Name three habits of confident delivery. 2) Why should you treat a tough question as a good sign rather than a threat? 3) What should you do when an investor asks something you genuinely do not know? 4) Should you answer a question the instant the investor starts speaking? 5) Rewrite this defensive reply into a calm one: ''That is not a real problem, our numbers are totally fine.'' Answers: 1) Any three of: rehearse until the structure is natural, slow your pace, make eye contact, keep an open posture, and pause before answering. 2) Because questions show the listener is engaged, and the Q&A is often where deals are decided. 3) Say plainly that you do not know, share what you do know, and promise a precise follow-up. 4) No - listen to the whole question and pause to think before you answer. 5) Something like: ''That is a fair concern. Here is what the data shows, and here is what we are doing about it; I can share the full chart afterward.''"},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading slides word for word, which reveals a lack of command and bores the room. Mistake: rushing out of nerves so the audience cannot follow. Mistake: getting defensive or argumentative during Q&A, which makes a small doubt look like a big one. Mistake: bluffing an answer you do not have, which destroys trust the moment the investor senses it. Fix these by rehearsing aloud, slowing down, treating questions as interest, and being honest about what you do not yet know. Exit ticket: (a) What is the single best response when you do not know the answer to a question? (b) Why is staying calm under a hostile question part of what investors are judging? Answers: (a) Admit you do not know, share what you do know, and offer a specific follow-up. (b) Because they are judging whether they trust you to lead a company through pressure, and the Q&A is a live test of exactly that."}]}'::jsonb, 'A question is interest, not an attack. Treat every hard question as a sign the listener is engaged.', 12),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"pitch deck","translation":"a short, ordered set of slides, usually ten to fifteen, that presents a startup to investors","example":"She walked the room through a twelve-slide pitch deck, from the problem to the ask."},{"term":"elevator pitch","translation":"a thirty to sixty second spoken summary of what your company does, meant to earn the next conversation","example":"His elevator pitch named the customer, the problem, and one result in under a minute."},{"term":"TAM, SAM, SOM","translation":"three layers of market size: the Total Addressable Market, the Serviceable Available Market, and the Serviceable Obtainable Market","example":"The market slide showed a large TAM, a narrower SAM, and a realistic SOM for the first three years."},{"term":"traction","translation":"evidence that customers are already using, paying for, or signing up for the product","example":"Their traction slide showed revenue doubling every quarter for a year."},{"term":"go-to-market","translation":"the plan for how a company will reach and win its customers","example":"The go-to-market plan started with direct outreach to clinics before any broad advertising."},{"term":"business model","translation":"how a company makes money - who pays, for what, and how often","example":"Their business model was a flat monthly subscription per clinic."},{"term":"the ask","translation":"the closing slide that states how much money is being raised and what it will achieve","example":"The ask was clear: one million dollars for twelve months of runway and fifty thousand users."},{"term":"runway","translation":"how many months a startup can keep operating before it runs out of cash","example":"At their current spending, the round bought about eighteen months of runway."},{"term":"demo day","translation":"an event, often ending an accelerator, where many startups pitch on stage to a room of investors","example":"She rehearsed her three-minute talk for a week before demo day."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (10, 'Demo day, tailoring the pitch, and common mistakes', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Adapt your pitch to the setting - especially the fast, high-pressure format of demo day - tailor it to the specific audience in front of you, and avoid the handful of mistakes that sink otherwise good pitches. By the end you will be able to take one core pitch and reshape it for a three-minute stage, a thirty-minute meeting, or a quick hallway chat, and you will carry a short checklist of errors to never make. Warm-up: think back over this whole course and, without looking, write down three mistakes you now know to avoid. This final lesson pulls the threads together. Earlier lessons built your story, your deck, and your delivery; here you learn to flex all three to the moment, because the same material can land brilliantly in one room and fall flat in another purely because of how it was shaped for the occasion."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Demo day is a showcase, often at the close of an accelerator, where a series of startups each take the stage for a very short slot, sometimes only two or three minutes, to pitch a room full of investors at once. That format rewards a tight, rehearsed, high-energy pitch that lands one clear idea and a memorable hook, because you will not have time for detail and the room is comparing you against many others. Tailoring is the broader skill behind this: before any pitch, ask who is in the room, how much time you have, and what they most care about, then adjust length, depth, and emphasis to fit. A technical investor may want to go deep on how the product works; a generalist may want the market and the numbers. A three-minute stage demands ruthless cutting; a long meeting invites discussion and detail. The core story stays constant, but its shape, its pace, and the slides you lean on all bend to the occasion in front of you."},{"heading":"Key parts & examples","text":"Picture the same clinic company in three settings. On a three-minute demo day stage, you deliver one crisp arc - problem, solution, one proof point, the ask - at high energy, with maybe six slides, and you leave them wanting the follow-up meeting. In a thirty-minute investor meeting, you walk the full deck, pause often for questions, and go deep on traction and the model. In a chance hallway conversation, you drop to the thirty-second elevator pitch and ask for a meeting. Now the common mistakes to avoid, drawn from every lesson so far: no clear problem; a feature list with no story; an inflated or missing market; hiding the competition; a vague ask; cluttered slides read aloud; rambling past your time; and getting defensive in Q&A. Keep that list as a pre-pitch checklist. Before you walk in, confirm you have a real problem, a story, an honest market, a clear ask, clean slides, a rehearsed delivery, and a plan for the hard questions."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) What makes the demo day format especially demanding? 2) Name three questions to ask yourself before tailoring a pitch. 3) How would you reshape a thirty-minute pitch for a three-minute stage? 4) List four common mistakes this course warned you about. 5) True or false: there is one perfect pitch that works for every audience. Answers: 1) You get only a few minutes on stage and are compared directly against many other startups, so the pitch must be tight, rehearsed, and memorable. 2) Who is in the room, how much time you have, and what they most care about. 3) Cut to one clear arc - problem, solution, one proof point, and the ask - at high energy with far fewer slides. 4) Any four of: no clear problem, no story, inflated or missing market, hidden competition, a vague ask, cluttered slides, rambling over time, defensive Q&A. 5) False - the best pitch is shaped for the specific person, format, and time available."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: delivering a long-meeting pitch on a demo day stage and running out of time before the ask. Mistake: giving every audience the same depth, so the technical investor is left shallow and the generalist is drowned in detail. Mistake: treating the pitch as finished once the deck looks good, without rehearsing it for the specific slot you were given. Mistake: ignoring the error checklist and repeating a known, avoidable blunder on the day it counts most. Fix these by asking who, how long, and what they care about before every pitch, by rehearsing in the real format, and by running the checklist one last time at the door. Exit ticket: (a) Name the one factor that most changes how you shape a pitch. (b) In one sentence, why is there no single perfect pitch? Answers: (a) The amount of time you are given, which sets how much you can say. (b) Because the right pitch depends on who is listening, in what format, and for how long, so it must be tailored every time."}]}'::jsonb, 'There is no single perfect pitch. The best pitch is the one shaped for the person, the format, and the time you are given.', 13)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0003-0000-4000-8000-000000000027' and l.position = v.pos
